On Tuesday, February 4, 2014 4:00:23 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > All the priors we have on symmetric positive definite matrices involve a determinant and as long as the exponent on that
> > determinant is positive, then they put zero mass on the singular matrices.
>
> Isn't the lkj_corr prior going to use an exponent of 0 or less for a
> scale nu = 1 or less?

Yes, but a number between 0 and 1 (the determinant of a correlation matrix) to a negative exponent is still a positive function that still integrates to a finite value over the space of correlation matrices of that size (iff eta > 0). It is almost analogous to a beta distribution with shape parameters equal and less than one (provided they are greater than 0).

Ben

