> That's it pretty much. The singular matrices are an edge case either way but in the absence of data, a chain would run
> there if 0 < eta < 1. For eta > 1, a chain would stay off the singular subspace, which accomplishes pretty much the same
> thing as a prior on the condition number.

I see what you mean about 0 < eta < 1 --- it's again like
beta(alpha,alpha) running to 0 or 1 for alpha < 1.  So even
though the edge cases have no measure, that's not the way they
behave given floating point arihmetic and priors favoring edges.

> The condition number is approximately monotonically related to the ratio of the biggest to smallest squared diagonal
> elements of the Cholesky factor. In the Wishart case, those squared diagonals are chi-squared marginally (with different
> degrees of freedom) and in the LKJ case, the biggest is 1 and the smallest is an asymmetric beta.

Cool.  One day I hope to understand all this more deeply.
I feel like a kid with a broomstick watching a Jedi master!
