On Tue, Feb 18, 2014 at 4:28 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Again, it depends on how you define Stan — is it
> a collection of libraries useful for Bayesian inference
> with some of those libraries dedicated to interfacing
> with external UIs, or is it an execution of Bayesian
> inference with supporting libraries?  In the former
> case I’d suggest ui, in the latter I’d go with core.

I see it as the first. stan-dev/stan is the collection of libraries. It doesn't really define an actual ui / executable.

On Tue, Feb 18, 2014 at 4:29 PM, Marcus Brubaker <marcus.a...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Of the listed options I like command, core or main may also be good.  As alternatives, how about inference_api or estimation_api?  Or is that too specific?

I think we should have one namespace / directory just so we don't end up cluttering src/stan. I think it makes sense to have inference and estimation as sub-namespaces, when we need them.

> On Tue, Feb 18, 2014 at 4:21 PM, Daniel Lee <bea...@alum.mit.edu> wrote:
> > How about "coreui"? This stuff isn't really the core of Stan.
> > Or maybe "shared"?
> >
> >
> > On Tue, Feb 18, 2014 at 4:04 PM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > So let’s say we go with Bob and everything is implicitly an API.
> > > That means we have an autodiff API, MCMC API, optimization
> > > API, etc, following our directory structure (also, I know
> > > realize that interface_api is stupid, like saying ATM machine).
> > > Then what this would be is the interface for Stan not as a series
> > > of libraries but as a unified platform for inference.  How about
> > > “core" for the basic interface libraries that tie everything together
> > > (the I/O, arguments, etc)?
> > >
> > > On Feb 18, 2014, at 8:32 PM, Daniel Lee <bea...@alum.mit.edu> wrote:
> > >
> > > > Back to naming. I've got one more: command.
